---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import { toolPath } from '../../i18n/config.js';

const locale = 'en';
const title = 'US Routing Number (ABA) Validator | Check and Validate';
const description =
  'Free US bank routing number checker. Validate a 9-digit ABA routing number with the official checksum and see its Federal Reserve region, entirely in your browser.';
const summary =
  'Enter a US bank routing number to check that it is nine digits and passes the ABA checksum. If it is valid, the tool shows the Federal Reserve region its leading digits point to. This checks the number\'s form, not whether the bank is real.';

const strings = {
  validHeadline: 'Valid routing number',
  invalidHeadline: 'Not a valid routing number',
  validNote: 'The number is nine digits and passes the ABA checksum. This confirms the form, not that it belongs to an active bank.',
  err: {
    empty: 'Enter a routing number to check.',
    format: 'A routing number is exactly nine digits. Enter digits only.',
    range: 'The first two digits are not in a range the ABA uses, so this cannot be a valid routing number.',
    checksum: 'The number fails the ABA checksum, so at least one digit is wrong or two are swapped.',
  },
  labelType: 'Region',
  regions: {
    regionPrimary: 'Federal Reserve Bank (primary)',
    regionThrift: 'Thrift institution',
    regionAch: 'Electronic transaction (ACH)',
    regionTraveler: "Traveler's cheque",
  },
};

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'Does a valid result mean the bank is real?',
    answer:
      'No. A pass means the nine digits are internally consistent under the ABA checksum, which is how a form catches a mistyped number. It does not confirm the number belongs to an open, active bank. Your own bank or the ABA registry can confirm that.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a routing number?',
    answer:
      'It is a nine-digit code that identifies a US bank or credit union in payments such as direct deposits, wire transfers, and cheques. It is sometimes called an ABA number, after the American Bankers Association, which introduced the system.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between a routing number and an account number?',
    answer:
      'The routing number identifies the bank; the account number identifies your specific account at that bank. On a paper cheque, the routing number is the first group of nine digits along the bottom, and the account number follows it.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why do routing numbers have a check digit?',
    answer:
      'The last digit is calculated from the other eight so the weighted sum lands on a multiple of ten. It exists to catch single wrong digits and most swapped pairs before a payment is submitted, not to prove the bank exists.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is my routing number sent anywhere?',
    answer:
      'No. The check runs entirely in your browser. Nothing you type is transmitted, stored, or logged.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout title={title} description={description} locale={locale} toolId="routing" summary={summary} faq={faq}>
  <Fragment slot="tool">
    <form class="calc-box" id="routing-form" novalidate>
      <div class="field" id="routing-field">
        <label for="routing-input">Routing number</label>
        <span class="hint">Nine digits. Spaces and dashes are ignored.</span>
        <input type="text" inputmode="numeric" id="routing-input" class="mono" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="123456780" />
        <span class="error-msg" id="routing-error"></span>
      </div>
      <button type="submit" class="btn">Validate routing number</button>
    </form>

    <div class="result-box" id="result-box" hidden>
      <div class="result-headline" id="result-headline"></div>
      <p class="result-note" id="result-note"></p>
      <ul class="info-list" id="result-info"></ul>
    </div>

    <script type="application/json" id="tool-i18n" set:html={JSON.stringify(strings)}></script>
    <script>
      import '../../scripts/routing-ui.js';
    </script>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="body">
    <h2>How It Works</h2>
    <p>
      A US bank routing number is nine digits, and the last digit is a check digit calculated from
      the other eight. That built-in relationship is what lets a payment form reject a mistyped
      number without contacting the bank. The first two digits also fall in set ranges tied to the
      Federal Reserve, which the tool reads to report a region.
    </p>
    <p>
      The checksum weights the digits in a repeating pattern of 3, 7, and 1, then checks that the
      total is a multiple of ten. Writing the nine digits as d1 through d9, the rule is:
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Group</th><th>Digits</th><th>Weight</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>d1, d4, d7</td><td>times 3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>d2, d5, d8</td><td>times 7</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>d3, d6, d9</td><td>times 1</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Add the three weighted groups together, and the number is valid when that total divides
      evenly by ten.
    </p>
    <h3>Worked Example</h3>
    <p>
      Take the sample number 123456780, which is made up rather than a real bank's. The first
      group is 3 times (1 plus 4 plus 7), which is 36. The second is 7 times (2 plus 5 plus 8),
      which is 105. The third is (3 plus 6 plus 0), which is 9. Adding them gives 150, a multiple
      of ten, so the number passes the ABA checksum.
    </p>

    <h2>When You Would Use This</h2>
    <p>
      Setting up a direct deposit, a bill payment, or a wire transfer means entering a routing
      number, and a single wrong digit can misdirect or bounce the payment. A quick checksum check
      catches that before it causes a problem. Because it is a US payment number, it pairs
      naturally with the <a href={toolPath('credit-card', locale)}>credit card validator</a>, which
      uses a similar check-digit idea.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
